A||||2||RICK REIGSTAD||OIL PRESSURE ON A V6 88GT
Z||000000||RICK REIGSTAD||05-12-99||08:15 PM||||WHEN I START MY CAR THE OIL PRESSURE IS 60-80<br>WHEN THE MOTOR IS COMPLETELY WARM AND I HAVE DRIVEN FOR AWHILE THE PRESSURE DROPS DOWN TO 10 AT ABOUT 800 RPM AT 2000 RPM THE PRESSURE WILL STAY AT ABOUT 35-40.HOWEVER I GET NERVOUS AT STOP SIGNS OR LITES OR SUDDEN HARD STOPS AND I LOOK OVER AT THE OIL PRESSURE GAGE AND IT IS READING 8-10  POUNDS OF PRESSURE. I KNOW IT IS NORMAL FOR A STANDARD US PRODUCTION CAR TO DROP TO 5 POUNDS OF OIL PRESSURE AT IDLE AND A HOT SUMMERS DAY, BUT A PREFORMANCE ENGINE LIKE THIS V6 GT? I DON'T THINK SO. COULD THIS BE AN AGEING OIL PUMP OR AGEING ROD AND CRANK BEARINGS. THERE IS 10,000 MILES ON THE TOP END THE BOTTOM END I DON'T KNOW. THIS MOTOR DOES NOT USE ANY OIL OR MAKE ANY NOISE. IT RUNS VERY STRONG. WOULD THE INSTALLATION OF A HIGH VOLUME OIL PUMP TAKE CARE OF THE LOW OIL PRESSURE READINGS AT IDLE WHEN WARM OR HOT.
Z||000001||Eric||05-12-99||08:41 PM||||I had the same problem on my 88 GT but at a much worse level.  The oil pressure gauge would read 10 pounds when first started and go down to about 2 pounds at running temp.  I changed the oil pressure sending unit on the engine but it didn't work.  However, I did have some luck when I unplugged the gauge from the dash wiring harness and plugged it back in.  This was only temporary.  I think the contacts could have been dirty or coroded.  It may be a good idea to try contact cleaner on the contact points.<p>From my experience, when the gauge is working properly, the cold temp pressure is around 45 pounds then backs down to around 25 pounds at running temp.  I'm not sure of idle pressure, but I imagine it would be about 15 pounds.  I think these cars have a fuel shut off command when there is not enough oil pressure so don't worry about toasting your engine.  In fact, I drove mine from Texas to Ohio with the gauge reading no higher than 5 psi at 80 mph.<p>I really don't think you have a problem with your 88.  I wished I could say the same for mine.
Z||000002||theogre||05-12-99||08:58 PM||||for safety,,, hook up a mechanical gauge to the motor, and make sure it's not doing the same thing..... If the mech does the same as the electonic, you have a major problem developing. Like maybe after a decade plus the oil pump is going. If the oil pan has ever been hit, or removed, you could also have a mangled pickup tube sucking in air.<p>The Fuel pump switch may funcion if there is any pressure at all.... I don't trust it to be a safe measure of pressure. I can't find a spec anyplace on it yet as to what point it activates at. I think may pull one off some time and test it.<p>even if you have to got buy a mech guage, they aren't that expensive.... it's cheap insurance.<br>
